The '''2020 EFL Cup final''' was the final of the [[2019–20 EFL Cup]]. It was played at [[Wembley Stadium]] in London, England, on 1 March 2020,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.efl.com/clubs-and-competitions/key-dates/|title=Key dates for the 2019/20 season|publisher=[[English Football League]]|access-date=19 December 2019|archive-date=31 May 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160531072553/http://www.football-league.co.uk/global/keydates.aspx|url-status=live}}</ref> and contested by [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]] and [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]]. It was Villa's first EFL Cup final since [[2010 Football League Cup final|2010]], and City's third successive EFL Cup final and fifth in the past seven seasons.

City won the match 2–1 to claim their third consecutive EFL Cup title. As winners, they would have entered the second qualifying round of the [[2020–21 UEFA Europa League]],<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.goal.com/en-us/news/carabao-cup-2020-final-watch-tickets-teams-time-date/e263pe564hkv1eip7jqpwl6a9 |title=Carabao Cup 2020 final: How to watch, tickets, teams, time & date |website=Goal.com |access-date=28 January 2020 |archive-date=28 January 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200128215331/https://www.goal.com/en-us/news/carabao-cup-2020-final-watch-tickets-teams-time-date/e263pe564hkv1eip7jqpwl6a9 |url-status=live }}</ref> but instead qualified directly for the [[2020–21 UEFA Champions League]] by finishing second in the [[2019–20 Premier League]]. Although they were initially banned from all UEFA club competitions for the 2020–21 and 2021–22 seasons by UEFA due to [[UEFA Financial Fair Play Regulations|Financial Fair Play]] breaches,<ref name="UEFA statement">{{cite news |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/025a-0f8e7535cab3-07272066f9f6-1000--club-financial-control-body-adjudicatory-chamber-decision-on/ |title=Club Financial Control Body Adjudicatory Chamber decision on Manchester City Football Club |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=[[UEFA|Union of European Football Associations]] |date=14 February 2020 |access-date=14 February 2020 |archive-date=27 February 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200227004659/https://www.uefa.com/insideuefa/about-uefa/news/newsid=2638659.html |url-status=live }}</ref> the decision was pending appeal to the [[Court of Arbitration for Sport]],<ref name="MCFC statement">{{cite news |url=https://www.mancity.com/news/club-news/club-news/2020/february/manchester-city-club-statement |title=Club statement |website=mancity.com |publisher=Manchester City FC |date=14 February 2020 |access-date=14 February 2020 |archive-date=27 February 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200227004659/https://www.mancity.com/news/club-news/club-news/2020/february/manchester-city-club-statement |url-status=live }}</ref> and overturned on 13 July 2020.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.tas-cas.org/fileadmin/user_upload/CAS_Media_Release_6785_Decision.pdf|title=13.07.2020 - Decision in the arbitration procedure between Manchester City FC & UEFA|publisher=Court of Arbitration for Sport|date=13 July 2020|access-date=13 July 2020|archive-date=5 November 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201105174215/https://www.tas-cas.org/fileadmin/user_upload/CAS_Media_Release_6785_Decision.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref>

