[[2019 EFL Cup final|EFL Cup holders]] Manchester City, as a Premier League team involved in the [[2019–20 UEFA Champions League]], started the competition in the third round. They were first drawn away at [[EFL Championship]] side [[Preston North End F.C.|Preston North End]]. At [[Deepdale]], City won 3–0 with goals from [[Raheem Sterling]], [[Gabriel Jesus]] and an own goal from [[Ryan Ledson]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Begley |first=Emlyn |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/49723452 |title=Preston 0-3 Manchester City: Holders ease through to fourth round |publisher=BBC Sport |date=24 September 2019 |access-date=29 January 2020 |archive-date=25 September 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190925205517/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/49723452 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the next round, they drew fellow Premier League team [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]] at home. At the [[City of Manchester Stadium]], they won 3–1 with two goals from [[Sergio Agüero]] and one from [[Nicolás Otamendi]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Bevan |first=Chris |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50134224 |title=Man City 3-1 Southampton: Sergio Aguero scores twice to put City into Carabao Cup quarters |publisher=BBC Sport |date=29 October 2019 |access-date=29 January 2020 |archive-date=19 February 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200219132454/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50134224 |url-status=live }}</ref> 

In the fifth round, they played against League One side [[Oxford United F.C.|Oxford United]] away at the [[Kassam Stadium]]. City won 3–1 with two goals from Sterling and one from [[João Cancelo]]. In the two-legged semi-final, they drew Premier League and [[Manchester derby|local rivals]] [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]]. City earned a 3–1 victory in the first leg at [[Old Trafford]], with goals from [[Bernardo Silva]], [[Riyad Mahrez]] and an own goal from [[Andreas Pereira]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Bevan |first=Chris |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50953053 |title=Carabao Cup - Man Utd 1-3 Man City: Holders overwhelm rivals in semi-final first leg |publisher=BBC Sport |date=7 January 2020 |access-date=29 January 2020 |archive-date=29 January 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200129104134/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50953053 |url-status=live }}</ref> Despite a 1–0 loss at home in the second leg, they reached the final with a 3–2 aggregate score.<ref name=manc>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/51201817 |title=Manchester City 0-1 Manchester United |publisher=BBC Sport |date=29 January 2020 |access-date=29 January 2020 |archive-date=29 January 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200129012526/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/51201817 |url-status=live }}</ref> City were looking to retain the EFL Cup for the third consecutive year, and win a seventh title overall.

==Match==

===Summary===
Manchester City manager [[Pep Guardiola]] made eight changes to his side, who had beaten [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] in the [[2019–20 UEFA Champions League|Champions League]] four days earlier, including leaving midfielder [[Kevin De Bruyne]] on the bench.<ref name="Telegraph">{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2020/03/01/aston-villa-vs-manchester-city-league-cup-final-live-score-latest/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200301222128/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2020/03/01/aston-villa-vs-manchester-city-league-cup-final-live-score-latest/ |archive-date=1 March 2020 |work=The Telegraph |date=1 March 2020 |title=Manchester City win Carabao Cup but plucky Aston Villa make them fight for 2-1 Wembley final |first=Jason |last=Burt}}</ref> Aston Villa manager [[Dean Smith (footballer, born 1971)|Dean Smith]] began with a defensive-minded 4–5–1 formation.<ref name="Guardian"/>

Manchester City dominated the first half of the game,<ref name="BBC">{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/51601970 |work=BBC Sport |date=1 March 2020 |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Aston Villa 1-2 Manchester City |access-date=1 March 2020 |archive-date=1 March 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200301183119/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/51601970 |url-status=live }}</ref> and they took the lead after 20 minutes; [[Phil Foden]] headed a chipped ball from [[Rodri (footballer, born 1996)|Rodri]] back across goal into the path of [[Sergio Agüero]], whose shot deflected off [[Tyrone Mings]] and into the goal.<ref name="Guardian">{{cite news |date=1 March 2020 |title=Manchester City sink Aston Villa to win third successive Carabao Cup |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2020/mar/01/aston-villa-manchester-city-carabao-cup-final-match-report |first=David |last=Hytner |work=The Guardian |access-date=2 March 2020 |archive-date=2 March 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200302000543/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2020/mar/01/aston-villa-manchester-city-carabao-cup-final-match-report |url-status=live }}</ref> Ten minutes later, they extended their lead from a corner kick, as [[Rodri (footballer, born 1996)|Rodri]] lost his marker, [[Frédéric Guilbert]], and headed past goalkeeper [[Ørjan Nyland]].<ref name="Independent">{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-league-cups/manchester-city-vs-aston-villa-result-report-score-sergio-aguero-rodri-samatta-a9367591.html |work=The Independent |date=1 March 2020 |title=Aston Villa vs Man City result: Sergio Aguero and Rodri secure hard-fought third successive Carabao Cup |first=Miguel |last=Delaney |access-date=1 March 2020 |archive-date=1 March 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200301203451/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-league-cups/manchester-city-vs-aston-villa-result-report-score-sergio-aguero-rodri-samatta-a9367591.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Reports noted that the corner that led to the City goal was unjustly awarded,<ref name="BBC"/><ref name="Telegraph"/><ref name="Guardian"/> but City remained dominant and enjoyed their strongest spell of the game in the 10 minutes following Rodri's goal. A comeback appeared a remote prospect,<ref name="Independent"/> but Aston Villa got a goal back against the run of play in the 41st minute, when [[John Stones]] slipped to allow [[Mbwana Samatta]] to score with a diving header.<ref>{{cite news |work=Metro |url=https://metro.co.uk/2020/03/01/gary-neville-reacts-john-stones-big-mistake-gift-aston-villa-goal-manchester-city-12330041/ |title=Gary Neville reacts to John Stones' 'big mistake' against Aston Villa |date=1 March 2020 |access-date=1 March 2020 |archive-date=1 March 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200301205321/https://metro.co.uk/2020/03/01/gary-neville-reacts-john-stones-big-mistake-gift-aston-villa-goal-manchester-city-12330041/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Manchester City played with less dominance in the opening 10 minutes of the second half, but regained control after De Bruyne was brought on in place of [[İlkay Gündoğan]].<ref name="Telegraph"/> They then remained the dominant team throughout most of the final half-hour, having the majority of the possession as well as being disciplined when they lost the ball, winning the ball back and preventing dangerous attacks by Villa.<ref name="Guardian"/> But Villa defended well, their players risking injuries to make important tackles,<ref name="Telegraph"/> and despite City's dominance they were unable to score a third goal.<ref name="BBC"/> Villa almost equalised in the 88th minute, when [[Björn Engels]] headed the ball towards the goal from a [[Conor Hourihane]] corner, but City goalkeeper [[Claudio Bravo]] pushed the ball onto the post to maintain his team's lead. Despite another corner in injury time, Manchester City held on for the 2–1 win, and lifted the trophy for the third successive year.<ref name="Guardian"/>

